`check face` judges a router's config file against the constrained faces
its face policy names (`link.v1` §2.15, #769). What a router runs is not on
the bus (core §11.3), so it reads a file, through zenoh's own loader, and
opens no session. For each face it reads the block as it guards the far
side: the denies of every policy whose subject can match the far
principal's sessions, subject by subject, since any allow wins across
subjects (§2.4), and the downsampling rules on the face's transports. It
reads every other subject too: zenoh ties no credential to a link, so a
subject bound to another principal that is not kept off the face's link
protocols matches that principal's credentials over the face (§2.4, 0.3),
and a subject bound to nobody is the face's own only when it selects the
face's link protocols alone and rides exactly the far principal's policies
(0.4). Then it compares the whole block with the plan the same face policy,
enrollment and contracts compile to, as `acl gen --check` does.

The lab router of `examples/zk2/link/`, running the block `acl gen
--face-policy` wrote: every face guarded as planned, exit 0. The windows
were not asked, and the note says so.

```console
$ zenctl check face tests/cmd/fixtures/link/router.json5 --face-policy ../examples/zk2/link/radio.face.toml --enrollment ../examples/zk2/link/radio.enrollment.toml --contracts ../examples/zk2/link/radio.v1.toml --format table
tests/cmd/fixtures/link/router.json5: face radio (far ground): 8 deny rule(s) across subject(s) ground; 2 downsampling rule(s) on its transports
PASS
judged against the config file tests/cmd/fixtures/link/router.json5 through zenoh's own loader: what a router runs is not observable on the bus, so a file that differs from what it was started with is invisible here (core §11.3, link.v1 §2.14)
tombstone windows not asked: pass --window ADDRESS=SECS for each listed owner of state (link.v1 §2.11)

```

The same block hand-edited the three ways a deployment must not: the `zk`
deny taken out of ground's policy, `local`'s subject left as 0.2 wrote it,
bound by its user name alone (F-118: its credentials over the face would
carry its grants there), and one wildcard rule in place of the two
(scenarios §3, step 2). All three are §2.15's findings, and the comparison
names the edits. Exit 1.

